   For more information see [MDL-89384](https://tracker.moodle.org/browse/MDL-89384)
+- A standard PHP API is now available for mounting React components. Feature code should only specify the component and its props; prop serialisation, escaping, and the DOM contract used by `core/react_autoinit` are handled internally.
+
+  ```php
+  // Via the renderer.
+  echo $OUTPUT->render_react_component('@moodle/lms/mod_book/viewer', ['title' => 'My Book']);
+
+  // Or via html_writer.
+  echo \core\output\html_writer::react_component('@moodle/lms/mod_book/viewer', ['title' => 'My Book']);
+  ```
+
+  A new `\core\output\react_renderable` interface has also been added. It works in a similar way to `named_templatable` and provides a migration path from templates to React. When a renderable implementing it is passed to `render()`, the renderer generates the React mount point using the component returned by `get_react_component()` and the props returned by `export_for_react()`. A `render_[renderable]` method on the renderer takes precedence over this interface, which in turn takes precedence over `named_templatable` and `templatable`.
+
+  For more information see [MDL-89296](https://tracker.moodle.org/browse/MDL-89296)
 
 ### Changed
 
diff --git a/public/lib/classes/output/html_writer.php b/public/lib/classes/output/html_writer.php
index 30b7b303ad7..7e04855e572 100644
--- a/public/lib/classes/output/html_writer.php
+++ b/public/lib/classes/output/html_writer.php
@@ -846,6 +846,64 @@ class html_writer {
         return self::tag('div', $content, self::add_class($class, $attributes));
     }
 
+    /**
+     * Creates a mount point for a React component.
+     *
+     * The generated element is picked up automatically by the `core/react_autoinit` module, which imports the
+     * specified component and renders it into the element with the supplied props.
+     *
+     * Feature code should only need to supply the component specifier and its props. The serialisation of the props,
+     * the escaping of the generated markup, and the DOM contract used by the React autoinit system are all handled
+     * here and should not be relied upon by consumers.
+     *
+     * @param string $component The fully-qualified component specifier, in the form `@moodle/lms/<component>/<module>`
+     * @param array|\stdClass $props The props to pass to the component. Must only contain JSON serialisable simple types.
+     * @param string $content Optional fallback content to display until the component has been mounted
+     * @param null|array $attributes Optional other attributes to add to the mount element (for example id, or class)
+     * @return string HTML code for the React mount point
+     * @throws coding_exception If the component specifier is not valid
+     */
+    public static function react_component(
+        string $component,
+        array|\stdClass $props = [],
+        string $content = '',
+        ?array $attributes = null,
+    ): string {
+        if (!str_starts_with($component, '@moodle/lms/')) {
+            throw new coding_exception(
+                "Invalid React component '{$component}'. Expected a specifier in the form @moodle/lms/<component>/<module>.",
+            );
+        }
+
+        $attributes = $attributes ?? [];
+        $attributes['data-react-component'] = $component;
+        $attributes['data-react-props'] = self::encode_react_props($props);
+
+        return self::tag('div', $content, $attributes);
+    }
+
+    /**
+     * Serialise the props for a React component.
+     *
+     * The props are always encoded as a JSON object so that they can be spread onto the React component.
+     *
+     * @param array|\stdClass $props
+     * @return string The JSON-encoded props
+     * @throws coding_exception If the props cannot be encoded
+     */
+    protected static function encode_react_props(array|\stdClass $props): string {
+        // An empty array would be encoded as a JSON list. Always encode props as an object.
+        if (is_array($props) && empty($props)) {
+            $props = new \stdClass();
+        }
+
+        try {
+            return json_encode($props, JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE | JSON_HEX_TAG | JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR);
+        } catch (\JsonException $e) {
+            throw new coding_exception('Unable to serialise React component props: ' . $e->getMessage());
+        }
+    }
+
     /**
      * Starts a <div> tag. (Shortcut function.)
      *
